Fixed. Your pip wasn't misconfigured or blocked — it was **missing**.

**Cause.** PATH resolves `/usr/local/bin/python3` → Python 3.13.1 (prefix `/usr/local`), and its `site-packages` held only `README.txt` and `pip-24.3.1.dist-info/` — the `pip/` package directory itself was gone. 841 of the 852 files in pip's RECORD were absent from disk. So every invocation died at the import, not at the network:

```
/usr/local/bin/python3: No module named pip
```

Two things this ruled out, since they're the usual suspects and both were *wrong* here: PEP 668 (`EXTERNALLY-MANAGED` exists only on the shadowed `/usr/bin/python3.11`, not on the active prefix), and permissions (you're root, the directory was writable). The 91%-full disk was incidental.

**Fix.** `python3 -m ensurepip --upgrade` — it reinstalls from the bundled wheel already on disk, no network needed.

**One trap worth knowing:** that command alone reported exit 0 while repairing nothing — `Requirement already satisfied: pip in /usr/local/lib/python3.13/site-packages (24.3.1)`. The orphaned `dist-info` made pip look installed. The stale directory had to be moved aside first, and only then did ensurepip really say `Successfully installed pip-24.3.1`. A green exit code on that step is not proof of a repair.

**Check that it works.** After the repair, RECORD matches disk again (852 entries, 0 missing), `pip`/`pip3`/`python3 -m pip` all print `pip 24.3.1 ... (python 3.13)`, and a real round trip succeeded: `pip install six` downloaded from PyPI, `import six` worked, then uninstall removed it cleanly. The stale `dist-info` backup and scratch files are deleted; `/usr/bin/python3.11` was not touched, and nothing was written into this repository (which contains only the `.gnomon/` harness surface — no Python code, which is why the diagnosis had to reach outside it).

If you later want packages that won't break this way, `python3 -m venv ~/.venv` is the sturdier habit — but the system prefix itself is healthy now.

